obligation.partial_result:
For a fixed index \(k\), define
\[
 L_k(t)=\min_{u\in\mathcal C_n,\,u_k=t}
        \sum_{i=1}^n(Z_{(i)}-u_i)^2.
\]
The feasible set is nonempty because it contains \(t\mathbf 1\).  If \(u^{(1)}\) and \(u^{(2)}\) are feasible at \(t_1\) and \(t_2\), then \(\lambda u^{(1)}+(1-\lambda)u^{(2)}\) is feasible at \(\lambda t_1+(1-\lambda)t_2\); convexity of squared loss and passage to infima show that \(L_k\) is convex.  Hence
\[
 Q_n(a,t)=L_{k(a)}(t)-\min_{u\in\mathcal C_n}
                  \sum_{i=1}^n(Z_{(i)}-u_i)^2
\]
is convex in \(t\), and each sublevel set \(\mathcal C_n(a)\) is an interval, allowing the empty interval and \(\mathbb R\).  Positivity of \(\widehat\kappa\) gives the exact event identity
\[
 \{\theta(a)\in\mathcal C_n(a)\text{ for every }a\in J\}
 =\left\{\sup_{a\in J}
     \frac{Q_n(a,\theta(a))}{\widehat\kappa(a)}
     \le\widehat q_{n,\alpha}\right\}
 =\{S_n(\theta)\le\widehat q_{n,\alpha}\}.
\]
On this event the monotone true curve is one of the selectors in \(\texttt{def:crossing-set}\), so its crossing value \(\tau_\zeta\) belongs to the image set, including the stated empty-set and endpoint conventions.  Thus inversion loses no coverage.  Its claimed limiting probability remains exactly the unresolved calibration probability in \(\texttt{thm:feasible-calibration}\).
